6️⃣ Residual Edge Cases (Final Hardening Points)

You are extremely close to a fully hardened system. There are only a few subtle edge conditions left.

⸻

🔶 A) Fingerprint Definition Is Slightly Underspecified

You say:

file length + last modified time, or content hash.

But last modified time can change without semantic change.

For deterministic governance, you ideally want:
	•	Content hash only (preferred)
	•	Or canonicalized content hash

Otherwise:
	•	A file rewrite with no change invalidates unnecessarily.

Not critical — but if this ever runs in CI, it matters.

⸻

🔶 B) Lock + Crash Scenario

Edge case:
	•	STEP 3.9 acquires lock.
	•	Process crashes before STEP 4.
	•	state.json shows lock acquired.
	•	Lock file exists.

On resume:
	•	Lock consistency check passes.
	•	System resumes normally.

Good.

But if crash happens after backlog write but before lock release:
	•	backlog updated
	•	lock file still present
	•	state.json may not show release

You correctly require:

If environment does not permit removing lock → record blocker and halt.

That protects you.

This area is safe.

⸻

🔶 C) Mutation After Publish?

Right now:
	•	Publish Gate sets publish_eligible=true
	•	Then state becomes Published

But what if:
	•	Someone edits stage3_execution_plan.md afterward?
	•	Or edits escalations.md?

Your RESUME PRECHECK will detect mutation.

But do you allow re-opening a Published cycle?

You did not explicitly forbid it.

Right now, resume rules would:
	•	Detect mutation
	•	Invalidate downstream steps
	•	Re-run integrity checks

Which means a Published cycle could become non-Validated.

That may be desirable — but it should be intentional.

You may want:

If status == Published and mutation detected → require explicit --mode strict and confirm re-opening intent.

Otherwise cycles are not immutable.

⸻

🔶 D) Multi-Release Overlap on Same Roadmap File

You allow roadmap annotation in STEP 5.

But no lock exists for:
	•	claude/roadmap/current_roadmap.md

If two cycles annotate concurrently, it could conflict.

Not critical — since annotation is append-only — but worth noting if scaled.

